course: dictionary
lesson: 13195
title: Fuelled: more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fuel is what a machine or a fire needs to run or burn, such as petrol, gas, coal or wood. The word fuelled comes from fuel. «The plane was fuelled up before it took off.» The sentence tells you the plane was given fuel so that it could fly.
explain: Fuelled can also mean made stronger or bigger, especially feelings, ideas or fights. «Anger was fuelled by rumours.» Rumours are stories that may not be true, so they made the anger grow. Ask yourself: is something being given fuel, or is something being made bigger? The sentence will show you which.
explain: Fuelled can join with another word to tell you what something runs on. «The bus is diesel-fuelled» means it runs on diesel. «Leo is coffee-fuelled this morning» means coffee gives him his energy. The hyphen joins the two words into one idea.
fact: Fuel is anything that a machine or a fire burns to make energy, such as petrol, gas, coal or wood.
fact: «Fuelled» means given fuel, so that it can work or burn.
fact: «Fuelled» can also mean made stronger or bigger, especially feelings, ideas or fights.
fact: With a hyphen, «-fuelled» tells you what something runs on, as in «diesel-fuelled» or «coffee-fuelled».
fact: In science, power stations can be coal-fuelled, gas-fuelled or nuclear, and burning fossil fuels adds carbon dioxide to the air.
exercise: «The tractor was fuelled with diesel before the morning work began.» What was the tractor given? | A new seat | Rain | *Diesel | Songs
exercise: «Rumours fuelled the argument between the two teams.» What did the rumours do? | *Made the argument bigger | Made the teams go home | Made the rain stop | Ended the argument
exercise: «The bus is diesel-fuelled, so it runs on diesel.» What does the word «diesel-fuelled» tell you? | It carries forty people | *It runs on diesel | It is painted red | It is very old
test: «The rocket was fuelled and ready for launch.» What does fuelled mean here? | Cooled down | *Filled with fuel, ready to go | Tied to the ground | Painted gold
test: «Her excitement was fuelled by the big win.» What does this suggest? | *Her excitement grew bigger | Her excitement stopped | Her excitement was sad | Her excitement was painted
exam: «The fire was fuelled with dry wood and roared up the chimney.» What does fuelled mean here? | Painted red | *Given fuel so that it could burn | Put out with water | Cooled by the wind
